2025-12-24 14:51:26 | INFO | Q332 id=gsm8k_mcq_00331 | baseline_pred=C | gold=C | prompt_variant=strong | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=True | raw=Based on the information provided, we can calculate the amount of taxes Nick would have to pay in each state for each job.\nFor Job A, Nick would earn $15 x 2000 = $30,000 before taxes. The total tax rate in the state is 20%, so Nick would pay $30,000 x 20% = $6,000 in taxes. This means that Nick would end up with $30,000 - $6,000 = $24,000 after taxes.\nFor Job B, Nick would earn $42,000 per year. The property tax in the state is $6,000, and there is also a 10% tax rate on net income after property tax. To find the total amount of taxes Nick would have to pay, we need to first calculate his taxable income. The taxable income is the gross income minus the property tax. In this case, the taxable income is $42,000 - $6,000 = $36,000. Then, we multiply the taxable income by the tax rate to get the amount of taxes Nick has to pay. In this case, the taxes Nick has to pay is $36,000 x 10% = $3,600. This means that Nick would end up with $42,000 - $3,600 = $38,400 after taxes.\nTherefore, the difference in net pay between Job A and Job B is $38,400 - $24,000 = $14,400. So, Nick would make $14,400 more at Job B than at Job A.\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) $14,400.
