2025-12-24 15:14:22 | INFO | Q1198 id=gsm8k_mcq_01197 | baseline_pred=A | gold=A | prompt_variant=strong | followup_pred=A | followup_correct=True | raw=Based on the information provided in the audio, we can calculate the cost of the scotch as follows:\n\n10 bottles of scotch * $600 = $6000\n\nWe are told that John also buys twice as many bottles of cognac as scotch, which costs 50% more per bottle. Let's assume the cost of each bottle of cognac is x.\n\nSo, the cost of 10 bottles of cognac is 10x.\n\nThe cost of twice as many bottles of cognac is 10x * 2 = 20x.\n\nWe are told that the total cost of all the bottles of scotch and cognac is $6000 + $20x.\n\nSince the cost of the 10 bottles of cognac is 10x, we can substitute this value into the equation above to get:\n\n$6000 + $20x = $6000 + $20(10x/10) = $6000 + $20x = $6000 + $200x\n\nSubstituting this value back into the original equation gives us:\n\n$6000 + $200x = $6000 + $200(20x/10) = $6000 + $400x\n\nSimplifying this equation gives us:\n\n$400x = $0\n\nTherefore, the cost of the 10 bottles of cognac is $0.\n\nSo, the total cost of all the bottles of scotch and cognac is:\n\n$6000 + $0 = $6000\n\nTherefore, John spends $6000 on everything. The answer is A.
