2025-12-24 15:46:40 | INFO | Q787 id=gsm8k_mcq_00786 | baseline_pred=A | gold=A | prompt_variant=medium | followup_pred=A | followup_correct=True | raw=Ricardo has 5 tomato plants that each yield 22 tomatoes for a total of 5 * 22 = 110 tomatoes. He also has 8 eggplant plants that each yield 4 eggs for a total of 8 * 4 = 32 eggs. In total, Ricardo will have 110 + 32 = 142 fruits from his garden. Therefore, the correct answer is A) 142.
2025-12-24 15:46:43 | INFO | Q789 id=gsm8k_mcq_00788 | baseline_pred=A | gold=B | prompt_variant=medium | followup_pred=B | followup_correct=True | raw=Based on the information provided in the audio, we can determine that the school will cover half the cost of the trip, which means that John will have to pay half of the remaining amount. The total cost of the trip is $300, so half of it would be $300/2 = $150. Since John already has $50, he will be missing $150 - $50 = $100.\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) 100.
2025-12-24 15:46:44 | INFO | Q859 id=gsm8k_mcq_00858 | baseline_pred=D | gold=B | prompt_variant=medium | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=False | raw=We can model the spread of the plague using a mathematical model called a compartmental model. In this model, we divide the population into different compartments based on their infection status. For example, there are three possible compartments:\n\n  * S: susceptible individuals who have not yet been infected\n  * I: infected individuals who are contagious\n  * R: recovered individuals who no longer carry the disease\n\nAt any given time, some fraction of the population is in each of these compartments. We can use the following equations to describe how the populations change over time:\n\n  * dS/dt = -βSI\n  * dI/dt = βSI - γI\n  * dR/dt = γI\n\nHere, dS/dt represents the rate at which new infections occur, dI/dt represents the rate at which infections progress to recovery, and dR/dt represents the rate at which recovered individuals leave the population.\n\n  * β is the transmission rate, which represents the average number of new infections per infected individual per day\n  * γ is the recovery rate, which represents the average number of recoveries per infected individual per day\n\nUsing these equations, we can calculate the number of infected individuals after three days as follows:\n\n  * At t=0, there are 10 susceptible individuals and none infected individuals.\n  * After one day, 10 * β = 10 * 6 = 60 new infections occur, so there are 10 + 60 = 70 infected individuals.\n  * After two days, there are 70 * β = 70 * 60 = 420 new infections occur, so there are 70 + 420 = 490 infected individuals.\n  * After three days, there are 490 * β = 490 * 420 = 2160 new infections occur, so there are 490 + 2160 = 2650 infected individuals.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) 2160.
