2025-12-24 16:06:48 | INFO | Q58 id=gsm8k_mcq_00057 | baseline_pred=C | gold=D | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=False | raw=The maximum number of boxes that can be loaded onto the truck while not exceeding the bridge's weight limit is 84.\nTo arrive at this answer, we need to first calculate the total weight of the boxes. Since each box weighs 15 pounds, the total weight of all the boxes is 15 pounds per box * 84 boxes = 1260 pounds.\nNext, we need to subtract the weight of the driver and the empty truck from the total weight of the boxes and the bridge. The combined weight of the driver and the empty truck is 3755 pounds, so the weight of the boxes and the bridge together is 1260 pounds + 3755 pounds = 4915 pounds.\nSince the bridge can carry no more than 5000 pounds, we cannot exceed this weight by loading any more boxes onto the truck. Therefore, the maximum number of boxes that can be loaded onto the truck is 84.\nTherefore, the correct answer is C: 84.
2025-12-24 16:06:51 | INFO | Q60 id=gsm8k_mcq_00059 | baseline_pred=C | gold=C | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=True | raw=The total number of raspberries in the bush can be found by adding the number of fruits in each cluster and the number of individual fruits scattered across the bush.\nEach cluster has 20 fruits, so there are a total of 6 * 20 = 120 fruits in all six clusters.\n加上分散在灌木丛中的67个单独的水果，所以总共有120 + 67 = 187个 raspberries。\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) 187.
2025-12-24 16:06:53 | INFO | Q63 id=gsm8k_mcq_00062 | baseline_pred=A | gold=B | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=False | raw=The information provided states that Marcy gets an annual pension of $50,000 for 40 years, starting after 20 years, she becomes entitled to 5% of the value of the pension per year. This means that after 20 years, her pension will increase by 5% each year.\nTo calculate her annual pension after 30 years, we need to determine how much her pension will be at the end of 30 years. We can do this by calculating the total amount of money that will go into her pension over 30 years, taking into account the 5% increase each year.\nFirst, let's calculate the total amount of money that will go into her pension over 20 years. Since she gets $50,000 per year for 40 years, and she only gets 5% of the value of her pension per year after 20 years, we can assume that her pension will increase by 5% annually.\nTo calculate the total amount of money that will go into her pension over 20 years, we can use the formula for compound interest:\nA = P * (1 + r/n)^(nt)\nWhere:\nA = final amount\nP = principal (initial amount)\nr = annual interest rate\nn = number of times interest is compounded per year\nt = time (number of years)\nIn this case, the principal is $50,000, the annual interest rate is 5%, and the number of times interest is compounded per year is 1 (since it's a yearly increase). The time is 20 years.\nPlugging in these values, we get:\nA = $50,000 * (1 + 0.05/1)^(1*20) = $68,909\nSo after 20 years, her pension will be $68,909.\nNext, we need to calculate the total amount of money that will go into her pension over the remaining 10 years. Since her pension will increase by 5% annually, we can assume that it will also increase by 5% annually over the next 10 years.\nTo calculate the total amount of money that will go into her pension over the next 10 years, we can use the formula for compound interest:\nA = P * (1 + r/n)^(nt)\nWhere:\nA = final amount\nP = principal (initial amount)\nr = annual interest rate\nn = number of times interest is compounded per year\nt = time (number of years)\nIn this case, the principal is $68,909, the annual interest rate is 5%, and the number of times interest is compounded per year is 1 (since it's a yearly increase).\nPlugging in these values, we get:\nA = $68,909 * (1 + 0.05/1)^(1*10) = $74,909\nSo after 30 years, her pension will be $74,909.\nTherefore, Marcy's annual pension after 30 years will be $74,909. Answer: C
