2025-12-24 16:08:23 | INFO | Q112 id=gsm8k_mcq_00111 | baseline_pred=C | gold=D | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=False | raw=The amount of water that the boat took on depends on how far Julia rowed from the shore. We know that it took her 16 seconds to row 20 feet, so her speed was 16 seconds/20 feet = 0.8 seconds/foot. We also know that the boat was taking on two liters of water for every ten feet she rowed back towards shore. Therefore, for every 10 feet she rowed towards shore, the boat took on 2 liters of water. To find out how much water the boat took on when Julia reached shore, we need to calculate how many 10-foot sections of the journey she made. Since the shore was 64 seconds away, this means she rowed for 64 seconds / 10 seconds/foot = 6.4 times. Therefore, the boat took on 6.4 times * 2 liters/10 feet = 1.28 liters per foot of rowing. Since Julia rowed 20 feet, the boat took on 1.28 liters/foot * 20 feet = 25.6 liters. Therefore, the boat had taken on 25.6 liters by the time Julia reached shore.\n\nTherefore, the answer is (C).
