2025-12-24 16:10:55 | INFO | Q186 id=gsm8k_mcq_00185 | baseline_pred=D | gold=D | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=D | followup_correct=True | raw=Based on the information provided in the audio, we know that Jenna picked half as many apples as her mom, who picked 20 apples. Therefore, Jenna picked 20 / 2 = 10 apples. Together, Jenna and her mom picked 20 + 10 = 30 apples. Therefore, the correct answer is D) 30.
2025-12-24 16:10:57 | INFO | Q191 id=gsm8k_mcq_00190 | baseline_pred=B | gold=B | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=B | followup_correct=True | raw=Based on the given information, John collects peaches for 3 hours at a rate of 2 peaches per minute. To find out how many peaches he collects, we need to multiply the number of minutes he collects peaches by the number of peaches he collects per minute.\nTherefore, the total number of peaches John collects is:\n3 hours * 60 minutes/hour * 2 peaches/minute = 360 peaches\nSo the correct answer is B) 360.
2025-12-24 16:11:01 | INFO | Q188 id=gsm8k_mcq_00187 | baseline_pred=D | gold=D | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=B | followup_correct=False | raw=The correct answer is A) 6.\nTo calculate the amount Mandy needs to pay to Benedict after 3 months with a monthly interest rate of 2%, we need to use the formula for compound interest:\nFinal Amount = Principal * (1 + r/n)^(nt)\nWhere:\nPrincipal = $100 Initial Interest Rate = 2% Number of Payments = 3\nPlugging in the values, we get:\nFinal Amount = $100 * (1 + 0.02/1)^(3*1) Final Amount = $100 * (1.02)^3 Final Amount = $100 * 1.02^3 Final Amount = $100 * 1.0489 Final Amount = $104.89\nSo, Mandy needs to pay $104.89 to Benedict after 3 months with a monthly interest rate of 2%. However, since Mandy only owes $100, she needs to add the remaining $4.89 to the principal amount before paying it to Benedict. Therefore, Mandy needs to give $6 to Benedict.
2025-12-24 16:11:03 | INFO | Q193 id=gsm8k_mcq_00192 | baseline_pred=C | gold=C | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=True | raw=The number of trees that Tom has left after 10 years can be calculated as follows:\n\nAt the beginning, Tom has 50 trees.\nAfter 1 year, he cuts down 2 trees, so he has 48 trees left.\nAfter 2 years, he cuts down another 2 trees, so he has 46 trees left.\nAnd so on, until after 10 years when he cuts down 2 more trees, leaving him with:\n\n46 - 2 = 44 trees\n\nHowever, 30% of these 44 trees will die, which means that 30/100 * 44 = 13.2 trees will die.\n\nTherefore, Tom will be left with 44 - 13.2 = 27.8 trees. \n\nSince we cannot have a fraction of a tree, we need to round this down to the nearest whole number. Therefore, Tom will have 27 trees left after 10 years. \n\nSo the correct answer is C) 27.
