2025-12-24 16:20:49 | INFO | Q506 id=gsm8k_mcq_00505 | baseline_pred=C | gold=D | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=False | raw=Based on the information provided, the most popsicle sticks Frederick can make if he buys the cheapest lumber is 1,200.\nTo see why, we need to determine how many popsicle sticks he can buy with his budget of $24. We know that he can get 200 sticks from a 2x4 piece of wood and 400 sticks from a 2x8 piece of wood. The total number of sticks he can get from these two pieces of wood is 200 + 400 = 600 sticks.\nHowever, we also know that the cost of each stick is different. A 2x4 costs $4, so each stick from a 2x4 costs $4/200 = $0.02. A 2x8 costs $6, so each stick from a 2x8 costs $6/400 = $0.015.\nTherefore, the total cost of all the sticks is 600 x ($0.02 + $0.015) = $90. This means that he has $24 - $90 = -$66 left over. Since he cannot have a negative amount of money, he cannot buy any more popsicle sticks.\nTherefore, the maximum number of popsicle sticks that Frederick can make with his budget of $24 is 600, which is equal to 1,200 sticks when divided by the cost per stick. Answer: C
