2025-12-24 16:27:53 | INFO | Q757 id=gsm8k_mcq_00756 | baseline_pred=D | gold=D | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=D | followup_correct=True | raw=Based on the information provided in the audio, the number of votes in favor of the new regulation cannot be determined with certainty. However, we can make some educated guesses based on the given information.\nFirst, we know that the vote passed with twice as many votes in favor of the new regulation as there were against it. This means that the number of votes in favor of the new regulation plus the number of votes against it equals 33 (since each person on the council gets one vote). Let's call the number of votes in favor of the new regulation x.\nSince the number of votes in favor of the new regulation is twice the number of votes against it, we can write the equation: x = 2y, where y is the number of votes against the new regulation.\nWe also know that the total number of votes cast was 33, so: x + y = 33\nSubstituting the value of x from the first equation into the second equation gives us: 2y + y = 33\nSimplifying the left side gives us: 3y = 33\nDividing both sides by 3 gives us: y = 11\nSo the number of votes against the new regulation was 11.\nUsing the equation x = 2y, we can find the number of votes in favor of the new regulation: x = 2(11) = 22\nTherefore, the number of votes in favor of the new regulation was 22.\nAnswer: D) 22
