2025-12-24 16:40:06 | INFO | Q1266 id=gsm8k_mcq_01265 | baseline_pred=B | gold=C | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=D | followup_correct=False | raw=Based on the information provided in the audio, Tim will need 30 lemons to make 4 gallons of lemonade. To calculate this, we can divide the number of gallons needed by the number of cups of lemon juice required per gallon. In this case, 4 gallons / 1 cup = 4 cups. Since each cup requires 6 lemons, we multiply 4 cups by 6 to get 24 lemons. However, Tim's best friend Allen asked for an extra gallon that is twice as tart as the other gallons. Therefore, Tim will need to double the number of lemons used for the regular gallons, which is 24 x 2 = 48 lemons. So, Tim will need a total of 24 + 48 = 72 lemons.\n\nTherefore, the answer is D) 72.
