2025-12-24 16:55:55 | INFO | Q397 id=gsm8k_mcq_00396 | baseline_pred=C | gold=B | prompt_variant= | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=False | raw=Based on the information provided in the audio, Jenny is dividing a pizza into 12 slices and giving 1/3 of the pizza to Bill and 1/4 of the pizza to Mark. If she eats 2 slices, then she has consumed 2 slices out of the total 12 slices. To find out how many slices are left, we need to subtract the number of slices that Jenny ate from the total number of slices.\nTherefore, the remaining number of slices can be found as follows:\nTotal number of slices = 12\nSlices eaten by Jenny = 2\nRemaining slices = Total number of slices - Slices eaten by Jenny = 12 - 2 = 10\nHence, there are 10 slices left if Jenny eats 2 slices.\nAnswer: C) 10
2025-12-24 16:55:58 | INFO | Q340 id=gsm8k_mcq_00339 | baseline_pred=A | gold=B | prompt_variant= | followup_pred=A | followup_correct=False | raw=Based on the information provided in the audio, we can reason that:\n\n  * Farmer Brown has 60 animals on his farm\n  * All the animals are either chickens or cows\n  * He has twice as many chickens as cows\n\nWe can use these pieces of information to calculate the total number of legs for the animals.\n\nLet x be the number of cows on the farm. Then, the number of chickens on the farm is 2x.\n\nSo, the total number of legs for the cows and chickens is:\n\n4x + 2(2x) = 4x + 4x = 8x\n\nSubstituting the value of x from the problem statement, we get:\n\n8x = 60\n\nSolving for x, we find that x = 7.5, which is not possible since we cannot have half a cow or chicken.\n\nTherefore, there must be an error in the problem statement or the information provided in the audio. Without further clarification, it is not possible to determine the correct answer with the given information.
