2025-12-24 17:22:42 | INFO | Q1245 id=gsm8k_mcq_01244 | baseline_pred=A | gold=C | prompt_variant= | followup_pred=A | followup_correct=False | raw=Based on the information provided in the audio, Audrey got 60% of the 70 questions on her first test correct, which means she answered 60/70=0.857 questions correctly. To move on to the seventh grade, she needs to correctly answer 70% of the total number of questions on both tests, which is 70+40=110 questions. If she got 60% of the 70 questions on her first test correct, then she needs to get 70*0.857=59.027 questions correct on the second test to move on to the seventh grade. However, since questions cannot be fractional, she needs to get at least 60 questions correct on the second test to move on to the seventh grade. Therefore, the closest choice is A) 40. Answer: A.
2025-12-24 17:22:43 | INFO | Q1318 id=gsm8k_mcq_01317 | baseline_pred=A | gold=B | prompt_variant= | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=False | raw=Based on the given information, we can set up the following equation:\n\n20x + 4y = 70\n\nWhere x represents the number of cows and y represents the number of chickens.\n\nWe can simplify the equation by dividing both sides by 2:\n\n10x + 2y = 35\n\nSubtracting 10x from both sides gives us:\n\n2y = 25\n\nDividing both sides by 2 gives us:\n\ny = 12.5\n\nSince we cannot have half an animal, we must round down to 12. Therefore, there are 12 chickens on the farm.\n\nAnswer: C) 12
