2025-12-24 18:07:55 | INFO | Q48 id=gsm8k_mcq_00047 | baseline_pred=A | gold=D | prompt_variant=correct | followup_pred=A | followup_correct=False | raw=To solve this problem, we can set up an equation using the information given in the audio. Let X be the number of red ties John buys, which is twice the number of blue ties. We know that the cost of red ties is 50% more than the cost of blue ties, so the cost of a red tie is 40 * 1.5 = $60\. Since John spends $200 on blue ties, we can write the equation:\n\nX * 60 + Y * 40 = 200\n\nwhere Y is the number of blue ties.\n\nWe also know that John buys twice as many red ties as blue ties, so X = 2Y.\n\nSubstituting X = 2Y into the equation above, we get:\n\n2Y * 60 + Y * 40 = 200\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n120Y + 40Y = 200\n\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\n160Y = 200\n\nDividing both sides by 160, we get:\n\nY = 1.25\n\nSo John buys 1.25 blue ties, which rounds down to 1 blue tie. This means he buys 2 * 1 = 2 red ties.\n\nSubstituting Y = 1 and X = 2 into the equation X * 60 + Y * 40 = 200, we get:\n\n2 * 60 + 1 * 40 = 200\n\nSolving for X, we get:\n\nX = 30\n\nSo John spends $30 on red ties.\n\nTherefore, the answer is A) 600.
