2025-12-24 18:08:00 | INFO | Q52 id=gsm8k_mcq_00051 | baseline_pred=D | gold=D | prompt_variant=incorrect | followup_pred=D | followup_correct=True | raw=We can start by finding the distance that Tom needs to travel each hour. We do this by multiplying his speed by the number of hours he sails. For example, when he sails from 1 to 4 PM, he will travel for 3 hours at a speed of 10 mph, so his total distance traveled during this time will be 3 * 10 = 30 miles.\n\nWhen he travels back, his speed is 6 mph. To find how long it takes him to travel 30 miles at this speed, we divide the distance by his speed. This gives us:\n\n30 / 6 = 5 hours\n\nTherefore, it takes Tom 5 hours to travel back from 4 PM to 1 PM. The answer is D) 5.
