2025-12-24 18:10:42 | INFO | Q148 id=gsm8k_mcq_00147 | baseline_pred=A | gold=C | prompt_variant=correct | followup_pred=B | followup_correct=False | raw=We can start by setting up an equation to represent the number of bees that left and returned over time. Let L be the number of bees that left in the first 6 hours, R be the number of bees that returned in the next 6 hours, and T be the total number of bees that left and returned.\n\nFrom the given information, we know that:\n\nL = 30\nR = L / 2\nT = 2 * L\n\nSubstituting the values for L and T into the third equation, we get:\n\nT = 2 * 30 = 60\n\nThis means that there were 60 bees that left and returned over the entire period of 12 hours.\n\nHowever, we are only interested in the last 6 hours of the day, so we need to find the number of bees that returned during this time period. To do this, we can subtract the number of bees that left in the first 6 hours from the total number of bees that left and returned:\n\nR = 60 - 30 = 30\n\nTherefore, Debra saw 30 bees return to the hive in the last 6 hours of the day.\n\nTherefore, the answer is (B).
2025-12-24 18:10:43 | INFO | Q142 id=gsm8k_mcq_00141 | baseline_pred=C | gold=C | prompt_variant=incorrect | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=True | raw=We can start by setting up an equation to represent the information given in the problem. Let x be the number of worker bees, y be the number of baby bees, and z be the number of queens. We know that:\n\nx = 2y\ny = 2z\n\nSubstituting the second equation into the first equation, we get:\n\nx = 2(2z)\nx = 4z\n\nSince there are 700 bees in the hive, we can set x equal to 700:\n\n4z = 700\nz = 175\n\nNow we know that there are 175 queens in the hive. Substituting this value back into either equation for y or x, we can find the number of worker bees and baby bees.\n\nFor example, using the equation x = 2y, we get:\n\nx = 2(175)\nx = 350\n\nSo there are 350 worker bees in the hive.\n\nTherefore, the answer is (C).
