2025-12-24 18:12:08 | INFO | Q193 id=gsm8k_mcq_00192 | baseline_pred=C | gold=C | prompt_variant=incorrect | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=True | raw=We can start by setting up an equation for the number of trees remaining after each year:\n\nNumber of trees remaining = Number of trees planted - Number of trees chopped down\n\nAfter the first year, there will be 50 - 2 = 48 trees remaining.\n\nAfter the second year, there will be 48 - 2 = 46 trees remaining.\n\nAnd so on, until after 10 years:\n\nNumber of trees remaining after 10 years = 46 * (1 - 0.3) = 46 * 0.7 = 32.2\n\nSince we cannot have a fraction of a tree, we round this down to 32 trees. Therefore, Tom has 32 trees left after 10 years.\n\nTherefore, the answer is (C).
