2025-12-24 18:13:34 | INFO | Q242 id=gsm8k_mcq_00241 | baseline_pred=B | gold=B | prompt_variant=incorrect | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=False | raw=We can start by analyzing the information given in the audio.\n\nDolly has two books.\nPandora has one book.\n\nThe problem states that both Dolly and Pandora read each other's books as well as their own. This means that each person reads a total of three books (one of their own and two of the other person's).\n\nTherefore, if we add up the number of books each person reads, we get:\n\nDolly reads 2 + 2 = 4 books\nPandora reads 1 + 2 = 3 books\n\nSo, together, Dolly and Pandora will read a total of 4 + 3 = 7 books by the end.\n\nHowever, since the question asks for the answer in the form of "X books", we need to round up to the nearest whole number. Therefore, the answer is 8 books.\n\nHence, the correct answer is not among the options given. The closest option would be C) 4, but that is incorrect because the actual answer is 8 books.
2025-12-24 18:13:36 | INFO | Q241 id=gsm8k_mcq_00240 | baseline_pred=A | gold=B | prompt_variant=correct | followup_pred=D | followup_correct=False | raw=Based on the given information, we can set up the following equations:\n\nJames = Robert + 6\nJohn = 2 * (Robert + 6)\nJohn = 2 * Robert + 12\n\nWe are given that John has 54 candies. Substituting this into the last equation, we get:\n\n54 = 2 * Robert + 12\n\nSolving for Robert, we find that Robert has:\n\nRobert = 21\n\nSubstituting this value back into either of the first two equations, we can find that James has:\n\nJames = 21 + 6 = 27\n\nTherefore, John has 54 - 27 = 27 - 21 = 6 more candies than James.\n\nTherefore, the answer is (D).
