2025-12-24 18:20:36 | INFO | Q501 id=gsm8k_mcq_00500 | baseline_pred=A | gold=B | prompt_variant=correct | followup_pred=A | followup_correct=False | raw=Based on the given information, we can set up the following equations:\n\nLily + David + Bodhi = 43\nLily = David + 7\nDavid = Bodhi/2\n\nSubstituting the second equation into the third equation, we get:\n\nLily = (David + 7) + (Bodhi/2)\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\nLily = David + 7 + Bodhi/2\n\nSubstituting the value of David from the second equation into this equation, we get:\n\nLily = (David + 7) + (Bodhi/2) = (David + Bodhi/2) + 7\n\nSubstituting the value of Bodhi from the third equation into this equation, we get:\n\nLily = (David + Bodhi/2) + 7 = (David + (Bodhi/2)) + 7\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\nLily = (David + Bodhi/2) + 7 = (David + Bodhi) + 7\n\nSubstituting the value of David from the second equation into this equation, we get:\n\nLily = (David + Bodhi) + 7 = (David + Bodhi/2) + 7\n\nSince the left-hand side of this equation is equal to the right-hand side of the previous equation, we can conclude that:\n\nDavid + Bodhi = 0\n\nThis implies that either David or Bodhi found zero insects. However, since the problem states that both David and Bodhi found some insects, it is not possible for both of them to have found zero insects.\n\nTherefore, the only possible solution is that David found zero insects and Bodhi found 43 insects. This means that Lily found 43 + 7 = 43 + 7 = 50 insects.\n\nTherefore, the answer is A) 50.
