2025-12-24 18:26:15 | INFO | Q706 id=gsm8k_mcq_00705 | baseline_pred=B | gold=B | prompt_variant=incorrect | followup_pred=B | followup_correct=True | raw=The given information states that Martha's cat is five times faster than her turtle. If the cat can run 15 feet per second, we need to find out how far her turtle can crawl in 40 seconds.\n\nTo solve this problem, we can set up an equation:\n\nTurtle's distance = Cat's distance / 5\n\nWe know that the cat can run 15 feet per second for 40 seconds, so:\n\nCat's distance = 15 feet/second × 40 seconds = 600 feet\n\nNow we can substitute this value into the equation to find the turtle's distance:\n\nTurtle's distance = 600 feet / 5 = 120 feet\n\nTherefore, the turtle can crawl 120 feet in 40 seconds.\n\nSo the correct answer is B) 120.
2025-12-24 18:26:15 | INFO | Q688 id=gsm8k_mcq_00687 | baseline_pred=A | gold=A | prompt_variant=incorrect | followup_pred=B | followup_correct=False | raw=The problem states that there is one set of twins and one set of triplets, and one twin is seven years older than one triplet. We can use algebra to solve for the age of one of the twins.\n\nLet the age of the triplet be x. Then, the age of the twin is x + 7. The total combined age of all three is x + (x + 7) + y = 44. Since we know that there are two sets of siblings, we can write this equation as 2x + 7 + y = 44.\n\nWe also know that the sum of the ages of any group of siblings must be odd, since each sibling contributes an odd number of years. Therefore, the sum of the ages of the triplet and the twin must be odd. This means that y must be odd.\n\nSubstituting y with an odd number, such as 9, gives us:\n\n2x + 7 + 9 = 44\n2x + 16 = 44\n2x = 28\nx = 14\n\nTherefore, the age of the triplet is 14 and the age of the twin is 14 + 7 = 21. So one of the twins is 21 years old.\n\nTherefore, the answer is (B).
