2025-12-24 18:28:29 | INFO | Q784 id=gsm8k_mcq_00783 | baseline_pred=C | gold=C | prompt_variant=incorrect | followup_pred=B | followup_correct=False | raw=Based on the given information, we can reason that:\n\n- Bob started with 7 fish in his pond, out of which 3 were orange and 4 were white.\n- He went to the pet store and had a sales assistant dip out 17 fish from a mixed tank containing both white and orange fish.\n- When he got the fish home and added them to his pond, he found that he now had twice as many orange fish as white fish.\n\nFrom this information, we can conclude that the number of white fish that Bob bought at the store is:\n\n- If we assume that each mixed tank contains an equal number of white and orange fish, then the total number of fish in the mixed tank is 7 (Bob's starting number of fish) + 17 (the number of fish Bob bought) = 24.\n- Since Bob now has twice as many orange fish as white fish, the number of white fish in his pond is 4 (his starting number of white fish) / 2 (twice as many orange fish) = 2.\n- Therefore, the number of white fish that Bob bought at the store is 24 - 4 = 20.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is not among the given choices.
