2025-12-24 18:45:06 | INFO | Q03 id=gsm8k_mcq_00564 | baseline_pred=C | gold=C | prompt_variant=incorrect | followup_pred=A | followup_correct=False | raw=We can set up an equation to represent the number of pages Judy read in two weeks as a function of her reading speed in pages per minute. Let x be the number of pages Judy read in two weeks. Since she read 15 minutes each night in the first week and 2 pages per 1.5 minutes in the second week, we can write:\n\n15x = 100\n\nSolving for x gives us:\n\nx = 66.67 pages per day (since 100 / 15 = 6.67)\n\nSince there are 2 weeks in the time frame we're considering, Judy read a total of 2 * 66.67 = 133.34 pages.\n\nTherefore, the closest choice is A) 140.
2025-12-24 18:45:10 | INFO | Q03 id=gsm8k_mcq_00660 | baseline_pred=A | gold=A | prompt_variant=incorrect | followup_pred=A | followup_correct=True | raw=We can start by setting up an equation for the number of sheep and geese on the farm. Let S be the number of sheep and G be the number of geese. We know that:\n\nS + G = 20 (total number of animal heads)\n4S + 2G = 70 (total number of animal legs)\n\nSubstituting the first equation into the second equation gives:\n\n4S + 2(20 - S) = 70\n\nExpanding and simplifying gives:\n\n4S + 40 - 2S = 70\n2S = 30\nS = 15\n\nTherefore, there are 15 sheep on Lee's farm.\n\nSo the answer is A) 15.
